ontracts as the ledger candidates to check for open-interest confirmation at the next open.</div></div><div class='sec'><h2>FINAL TAKEAWAYS</h2><ol class='naw'><li>The day&#x27;s defining institutional theme was a macro relief bounce layered on top of extreme single-name dispersion. Lower long-end yields helped stabilize equities, but the most important tape action was the <em>MRNA</em> shock and repeated <em>TSLA</em> long-dated upside calls.</li><li>The strongest bullish positioning in the visible clean single-stock options tape was <em>TSLA</em> via repeated <em>2027 470C</em> and <em>2028 600C</em> late-session call buying patterns. The timing and repetition suggest a possible coordinated program, though that remains unconfirmed.</li><li>The strongest bearish or defensive single-name options read in the visible sample was not a massive premium event but rather the cluster of <em>MRNA</em> downside puts after the stock&#x27;s historic surge, plus smaller healthcare and China ADR put activity. That makes the bearish tape more reactive than proactive.</li><li>The most important volatility trade was <em>MRNA</em>. The stock&#x27;s move, huge relative volume, elevated IV, and both bullish and bearish premium totals indicate a volatility event first and a directional event second.</li><li>The most important dark-pool event in the visible sample was the late-session cluster led by <em>GH</em> and <em>PIPR</em> on a relative-volume basis, while <em>BA-A</em> led absolute notional among the visible rows. None can be called accumulation or distribution from the available data alone.</li><li>Flow-versus-price divergences mattered. <em>XLE</em> carried larger call premium than put premium while finishing <em>-0.16%</em>, and gold surged <em>+3.33%</em> even as <em>SPY</em> closed <em>+0.19%</em>. That combination argues the tape remained cautious under the surface.</li></ol><div class='sub'>Session Scoreboard</div><div class='kv'><div class='item'><div class='k'>BULLISH FLO